**Purpose:** Define exactly how AEGIS moves from architecture to a working, testable, deployable product.

---

# 01. Purpose

This document defines the implementation strategy for AEGIS.

It answers:

> How do we build AEGIS correctly from an empty or clean repository into a functioning high-stakes healthcare and biosecurity decision-support system?

This document converts the previous architecture documents into an ordered engineering execution system.

It establishes:

```text
what gets built
in what order
by which layer
with which dependencies
with which validation gates
using which development tools
```

The objective is to prevent:

```text
feature drift
architecture drift
AI hallucination
duplicate implementation
broken state
inconsistent components
premature backend complexity
premature AI integration
screen-by-screen isolation
```

---

# 02. Core Implementation Principle

AEGIS must be built **progressively from foundations upward**.

The implementation hierarchy is:

```text
ARCHITECTURE
     ↓
DOMAIN
     ↓
DATA
     ↓
SERVICES
     ↓
STATE
     ↓
APPLICATION
     ↓
INTERFACE
     ↓
INTELLIGENCE
     ↓
INTEGRATION
     ↓
TESTING
     ↓
DEPLOYMENT
```

The team must not reverse this order casually.

---

# 03. The AEGIS Build Law

The following rule is mandatory:

> **Never ask an AI coding agent to implement something whose architectural contract has not already been defined.**

For example:

Wrong:

```text
"Build the Threat Intelligence screen."
```

Correct:

```text
Architecture
 ↓
Domain model
 ↓
Data contract
 ↓
API contract
 ↓
State transitions
 ↓
Screen specification
 ↓
Component specification
 ↓
Implementation
 ↓
Tests
```

AI tools should execute established architecture.

They should not invent architecture during implementation.

---

# 04. Source of Truth Hierarchy

When documents conflict, the following authority order applies:

```text
1. AEGIS Master Architecture
2. AEGIS Domain Model + Global State Architecture
3. AEGIS User Flow + Operational Journey Architecture
4. AEGIS Functional Requirements + 13 Screen Master Specification
5. AEGIS Interaction + State Transition Specification
6. AEGIS Data Architecture + Data Contracts
7. AEGIS API Architecture + Service Contracts
8. AEGIS Frontend Architecture + Application Architecture
9. AEGIS Design System + UI Component Architecture
10. AEGIS Security Architecture
11. AEGIS AI Intelligence Architecture
12. AEGIS Observability Architecture
13. AEGIS Threat/Risk Architecture
14. AEGIS Testing Architecture
15. AEGIS Infrastructure Architecture
16. This Development + Implementation Architecture
```

If implementation reveals a genuine contradiction, stop and resolve the architecture.

Do not silently patch around it.

